ce:section.ce:para:
We isolated 309 strains from two PA4 film surfaces that were submerged in a marine environment. Among the isolated strains, 11 strains formed clear zones on PA4 agar plates. Based on their 16S rDNA sequences, all selected PA4-degrading bacteria were presumed to be classified into Pseudoalteromonas spp. (Fig. 1
                        ). The biochemical characteristics of strain No. 5, which showed the highest PA4-degrading activity in culture supernatant (Fig. 1), are summarized in Table 1
                        . This strain was aerobic, gram-negative, motile, and rod-shaped (Fig. S1). The 16S rDNA nucleotide sequence of this strain showed 99.3% identity with that of Pseudoalteromonas issachenkonii. However, the biochemical characteristics of strain No. 5 did not match those of P. issachenkonii as strain No. 5 could not utilize various sugars, such as d-galactose and d-fructose [28]. Therefore, we concluded that strain No. 5 belongs to the genus Pseudoalteromonas and named it Pseudoalteromonas sp. Y-5.

The purification process is described in Table 2
                        . Approximately 5.3-fold purification was achieved, with an overall yield of 54%. The purified enzyme produced a single protein band (75 kDa) on SDS-PAGE (Fig. 3
                        ). The molecular mass of the native enzyme was estimated to be 130 kDa using a gel filtration column, suggesting that the enzyme consists of a homodimer. When the purified enzyme and PA4 film (1 × 2 cm) were incubated in artificial seawater at 30 °C for 24 h, the PA4 film was remarkably degraded (Fig. S3), suggesting that the purified enzyme degrades not only amorphous PA4 but also crystalline PA4.
